"Stand under a tall tree and look up. Every leaf at the top needs water, and all of that water came in through the roots, far below your feet. Your heart pumps blood around your body. A tree has no heart. So how does the water get up there? You might guess that the tree sucks it up, the way you drink through a straw. But sucking has a limit. Hundreds of years ago, people who built pumps found that suction could not raise water more than about ten meters, however hard they tried. Many trees are much taller than that. The answer starts in the leaves. A leaf is covered with tiny pores. They open in the daytime to let in the gas the leaf needs for making food. While they are open, water inside the leaf evaporates and drifts out into the air. Here is the important part. Water clings to itself very strongly. Inside a tree the water fills very narrow tubes, and it forms one unbroken column from the leaves all the way down to the roots. When a little water leaves at the top, it pulls the whole column up behind it, like a chain being drawn up through a pipe. So the tree does not spend energy to lift the water. The sun and the air do the work by drying the leaves. On a bright, dry day the pull is strong. On a damp day it is weak, and at night it nearly stops. A tree uses only a tiny part of this water to grow. Nearly all of it, from 97 to more than 99 parts in every hundred, passes straight through and out into the air. That may sound like waste, but the stream carries minerals up from the soil, and the evaporation cools the leaves.": "3de955214e60",
